{
 "text": "six monsters where the name Tyranitar. One of them eats mountains for breakfast. One is a rocket trapped in a cocoon. One is a robot from a billion years in the future. And one is so strong, the anime had to invent a machine just to control it. Today, I'm explaining every single one, weakest to strongest, and at the end, I'm ranking them. And trust me, the number one spot is not even close. Let's start at the bottom of the mountain, literally. Larvitar is born deep underground in total darkness, and it has never seen its parents' faces. To reach the surface, it has to eat its way out. The Pok\u00e9dex says, Larvitar feeds on soil, and after it has eaten a large mountain, a large mountain, it falls asleep so it can grow. That's the whole childhood. Born underground, eat a mountain, take a nap. And here's the part that gets me. Until it eats its way to the surface, a Larvitar has never seen its own parents' faces. The Pok\u00e9dex actually says that. It digs and eats and digs for its whole life just to meet them. In the anime, Ash carried a Larvitar all through Johto, and its mother was a Tyrannatar so protective that an entire episode is just everyone trying not to get crushed by her. And in gold and silver, the game's hired Larvitar in exactly one place, Mount Silver, the final mountain, home of the strongest trainer in the world. The game puts the baby at the top of the world. Remember that, because the baby that eats mountain grows into the thing that knocks them down. At level 30, Larvitar becomes Pewpitar. And Pewpitar is where this line gets weird. It's a cocoon. It can't walk, it has no legs it can use, and it is still one of the most dangerous things on the mountain. Its shell is as hard as bedrock. Its thrashing can topple a mountain. That's the Pok\u00e9dex's words, not mine. And it moves by building up gas inside its body and blasting it out under pressure, launching itself like a rocket. A rocket. This is a cocoon with a jet engine. And it's not even empty in there. The Pok\u00e9dex says it already has arms and legs forming under the shell. It's not sleeping, it's loaded. But here's the catch. Pewpitar is the longest weight in the family. It doesn't evolve until level 55. One of the latest evolutions of any Pok\u00e9mon ever. You carry this angry rock for 25 levels. And then the mountain wakes up. Level 55, Tyranitar. The armor Pok\u00e9mon. 600 total stats, which puts it in the pseudo-legendary club. The strongest non-legendary Pok\u00e9mon of its generate. And notice what changed. Larvitar and Pewpitar are rock and ground. Tyranitar is rock and dark. When the cocoon finally breaks, it loses the ground type entirely. The mountain doesn't just wake up, it turns to the dark side. And the Pok\u00e9dex does not hold back. If it rampages, it knocks down mountains and buries rivers. Maps must be redrawn afterward. They have to redraw the map. Its ability is sandstream, summons a sandstorm the second it enters battle. No setup, no warning. The weather itself works for Tyranitar. The storm grinds down everything that isn't built for it. And it even makes rock types tougher while it rages. That's why for over 20 years, Tyranitar has been one of the most feared picks in competitive battles. Entire teams are built around this one Pok\u00e9mon's weather. And in the Celebi movie, a villain catches one with a dark ball. A ball that forces Pok\u00e9mon to turn evil. And this is what that looks like. A Tyranitar that can't hold back tearing a forest apart, fighting Suikun to a standstill. That's the baseline. That's the normal one. Hold on to that, because everything from here is a remake. Quick detour, because nobody counts this one, and it's my favorite. In Black 2 and White 2, you can visit Pok\u00e9Star Studios, a movie studio, and fight a giant robot Tyranitar, Mecha Tyranitar. It's a movie prop. It is not a real Pok\u00e9mon, and it's still got two versions, MT and MT2, the second one upgraded with electric power. Game Freak built a robot Tyranitar as a joke in 2012. Keep that in your head, because 11 years later, they did it for real. But first, the darkest version. In Pok\u00e9mon Colosseum, the final boss doesn't use a normal Tyranitar. He uses a shadow Tyranitar. A Pok\u00e9mon whose heart has been artificially closed and turned into a fighting machine. And in Pok\u00e9mon Go, Shadow Tyranitar is a glass cannon. It hits 20% harder than the normal one, but it also takes 20% more damage. Same monster, no break, no armor. In Colosseum, you can save a shadow Pok\u00e9mon and purify its heart. But in Go, most trainers keep this one shadow on purpose, because a Tyranitar that hits this heart is worth the damage it takes. This is Tyranitar with the safety off. Now the robot. For real this time, Iron Thorns from Pok\u00e9mon Violet, a paradox Pok\u00e9mon that looks like somebody rebuilt Tyranitar out of steel and lightning a billion years from now. And I mean that literally, its own Pok\u00e9dex entry says it matches a Pok\u00e9mon described in a dubious magazine as a Tyranitar from one billion years into the future as a dubious magazine. Even the Pok\u00e9dex is roasting it. It's a rock and electric type with 570 total stats and an ability called Quark Drive that supercharges it on electric terrain. In the games, you find it deep inside Area Zero, a giant crater hiding Pok\u00e9mon from the far future. The movie prop became canon. The robot Tyranitar is real, it's here, and it is not the strongest one. Because the strongest one was hiding in 2003, Mega Tyranitar. When Tyranitar mega evolves, its total stats jump from 600 to 700, a number almost no Pok\u00e9mon ever reaches. Its attack stat alone is 164, higher than most legendaries. And unlike most mega evolutions, it doesn't trade its ability away. Sandstream still fires the second it steps in. It keeps the storm and gets the power. The armor on its back bursts open into spike, like the power doesn't fit inside anymore. In the anime, Elaine's Mega Charizard X, one of the strongest Pok\u00e9mon in the whole show had to go all out just to trade blows with one. And in the Volcanian movie, villains built a device called Mega Wave that forces Pok\u00e9mon to mega evolve against their will. And of course, their army included a mega Tyranitar. Because if you're building an army of forced mega evolutions, this is your tank hundred stat. A walking mountain wearing a sandstorm, there was never a contest. So here's the official ranking. Weakest to Stronget. Number six, L'Arvitar. It eats a mountain, but it's still a baby. Number five, pupitar. A rocket cocoon is terrifying, but it's still a cocoon. Number four, shadow Tyranitar. Harder hits, weaker guard, pure rage, no breaks. Number three, iron thorns. The future is scary, but 570 is not six on. Number two, Tyranitar. The original mountain. And number one, by a landslide, mega Tyranitar. 700 stat. It was never close. Now it's your turn. Which form should get its own full video next? L'Arvitar, pupitar, Tyranitar, shadow, iron thorns, or mega, comment the form and the most votes wins. And next episode, every dragonite explained and ranked, because this mountain is just the start.",
